One data structure that can be used to store information about a user's medical condition is an object or dictionary in the form of a patient health record. This can contain key-value pairs that hold information about the user, including their name, age, gender, and medical conditions. One of the keys could be "medical_conditions", the value of which is a list or array of the user's medical conditions. Each element in the list could be another object or dictionary, which contains information about the specific medical condition such as the diagnosis date, symptoms, treatment plans, medications, and notes from the physician. 

An example of such a data structure would be: 

{
    "name": "Jane Doe",
    "age": 45,
    "gender": "Female",
    "medical_conditions": [
        {
            "condition": "Diabetes",
            "diagnosis_date": "05/03/2015",
            "symptoms": ["frequent urination", "increased thirst", "fatigue"],
            "treatment": {
                "medication": ["metformin"],
                "insulin": true,
                "diet": "low-carb diet", 
                "exercise": "regular exercise"
            },
            "physician_notes": "Patient has well-managed diabetes with regular checkups and medication."
        },
        {
            "condition": "Hypertension",
            "diagnosis_date": "01/15/2018",
            "symptoms": ["high blood pressure", "headaches", "dizziness"],
            "treatment": {
                "medication":["lisinopril"],
                "lifestyle_changes":["low-sodium diet", "regular exercise"],
            },
            "physician_notes": "Patient needs to monitor blood pressure regularly and continue with medication and lifestyle changes."
        }
    ]
}

This type of data structure allows for easy addition, deletion, and updating of the user's medical conditions and provides a comprehensive view of their health history.

Here is one example of CSS code that provides an animation on hover:

```css
.my-hover-element {
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  background: #3498db;
  transition: all 0.5s;
}

.my-hover-element:hover {
  background: #9b59b6;
  transform: scale(1.5);
}
```

This code creates an element with a class `my-hover-element` and sets its width, height, and background color. It also sets a `transition` property, which specifies how long the animations should take when triggered. In this case, the animations will take 0.5 seconds to complete.

The second set of CSS rules target the `:hover` state of the `.my-hover-element` class. When a user hovers their mouse over an element with this class, its background color will change, and it will be scaled up to 150% of its original size. This creates an attention-grabbing animation that could be used, for example, to make buttons more interesting or to draw attention to important elements on a webpage.

Of course, this is just one example of an animation that you could create on hover - there are countless possibilities! You could experiment with other animations, such as rotating or skewing the element, changing its opacity, or animating its position on the page.

To showcase a product using HTML and CSS, we can create a simple web page structure using HTML tags and style it with CSS. Here is an example code that could be used to showcase a product:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Product Showcase</title>
  <style>
      body {
        font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      }
      .container {
        width: 80%;
        margin: auto;
      }
      .product-image {
        width: 100%;
      }
      .product-info {
        margin-top: 20px;
        text-align: center;
      }
      .product-name {
        font-size: 24px;
        font-weight: bold;
      }
      .product-description {
        margin-top: 10px;
      }
      .product-price {
        margin-top: 20px;
        font-size: 18px;
      }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <img class="product-image" src="product-image.jpg" alt="Product Image">
    <div class="product-info">
      <div class="product-name">Product Name</div>
      <div class="product-description">
        Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua.
      </div>
      <div class="product-price">$59.99</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we create a basic structure for the web page, including a container to hold the product image and information. Within this container, we create an `img` element to display the product image, and a `div` element with the class "product-info" to hold the product name, description, and price. 

We style the page using CSS, including setting the width of the container, centering the text, and adding margins to create space between the elements. We also apply styles to the font to make the product name bold and increase the size of the price. 

This example can be modified by changing the content of the product name, description, and price, as well as adding additional elements or styles as desired. The image source should also be updated with the appropriate source for the desired product image.

