One example of a data structure for a school management system could be a nested dictionary. This allows for easy and organized access to information about students, teachers, classes, and other aspects of the school. Here's an example:

```
school = {
    "students": {
        100: {
            "name": "John Doe",
            "age": 15,
            "grade": 9,
            "classes": ["Math", "English", "Science", "History"]
        },
        101: {
            "name": "Jane Smith",
            "age": 14,
            "grade": 9,
            "classes": ["Math", "English", "Science", "History"]
        }
    },

    "teachers": {
        50: {
            "name": "Mr. Jones",
            "age": 35,
            "subject": "Math",
            "classes_taught": ["Algebra 1", "Geometry"]
        },
        51: {
            "name": "Mrs. Brown",
            "age": 42,
            "subject": "English",
            "classes_taught": ["English 9", "English 10"]
        }
    },

    "classes": {
        "Math": {
            "teacher": "Mr. Jones",
            "students": [100, 101],
            "room": 105
        },
        "English": {
            "teacher": "Mrs. Brown",
            "students": [100,101],
            "room": 107
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, information about students, teachers, and classes are organized into separate sections, with each entry given a unique identifier (e.g. student ID or teacher ID). Within each entry are various attributes (e.g. name, age, classes taught), along with lists and sub-dictionaries as needed to further organize information. This allows for easy access to information and the ability to easily update or add new entries as needed.
